@subsection Deductive Information Retrieval

Logic programming excels in providing interfaces to data bases for information
retrieval.  The query language we shall implement in this chapter is designed
to be used in this way.

In order to illustrate what the query system does, we will show how it can be
used to manage the data base of personnel records for Microshaft, a thriving
high-technology company in the Boston area.  The language provides
pattern-directed access to personnel information and can also take advantage of
general rules in order to make logical deductions.

@subsubheading A sample data base

The personnel data base for Microshaft contains @newterm{assertions} about
company personnel.  Queries are domain data built from host constructors, so
the session builds its data base with @code{assertFact} and @code{addRule} on
one shared @code{QueryDatabase}, and asks its questions through a
@code{QueryDriver} whose answers render with @code{renderAnswer}:

@example kotlin
val db = QueryDatabase()

val driver = QueryDriver.streaming(db)

fun ask(query: QQuery,
    names: List<String>): List<String> @{
    val vars = names.map @{ name -> QVar(name) @}
    return driver.run(query,
        vars).flatMap @{ frame -> renderAnswer(frame,
            vars) @}.toList()
@}

fun headName(term: QTerm): String =
    when (term) @{
        is QSym -> term.name
        is QList -> term.items.firstOrNull()?.let @{ headName(it) @} ?:
            ""
        is QVar -> "?"
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Two engine facts shape every data choice below.  Unification returns a new
frame on a match and null on a mismatch, never a marker value.  A @code{QList}
tail is a unification term: common items match first, and any remaining suffix
must be accepted by the shorter list's tail; closed lists must match in length.

@noindent
A fact is a @code{QFact} over @code{QTerm} data: @code{QSym} atoms,
@code{QVar} pattern variables, and @code{QList} proper and improper lists.
Here is the information about Ben Bitdiddle, the resident computer wizard:

@example kotlin
