@subsubheading Simple queries

The query language allows users to retrieve information from the data base by
posing queries in response to the system's prompt.  For example, to find all
computer programmers one can say

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("job"),
                QVar("x"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                    QSym("programmer")),
                    null),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => ?x = [Hacker, Alyssa, P]
//    ?x = [Fect, Cy, D]
@end example

@noindent
The input query specifies that we are looking for entries in the data base that
match a certain @newterm{pattern}.  In this example, the pattern specifies
entries consisting of three items, of which the first is the literal symbol
@code{job}, the second can be anything, and the third is the literal list
@code{(computer programmer)}.  The ``anything'' that can be the second item in
the matching list is specified by a @newterm{pattern variable}, @code{?x}.  The
general form of a pattern variable is a symbol, taken to be the name of the
variable, preceded by a question mark.  We will see below why it is useful to
specify names for pattern variables rather than just putting @code{?} into
patterns to represent ``anything.''  The system responds to a simple query by
showing all entries in the data base that match the specified pattern.

A pattern can have more than one variable.  For example, the query

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(listOf(QSym("address"),
                QVar("x"),
            QVar("y")),
            null),
    ),
    listOf("x", "y"),
)
// => the nine employees' addresses, in data-base order,
// one
//    ?x = @dots{} / ?y = @dots{} pair per line
@end example

@noindent
will list all the employees' addresses.

A pattern can have no variables, in which case the query simply determines
whether that pattern is an entry in the data base.  If so, there will be one
match; if not, there will be no matches.

The same pattern variable can appear more than once in a query, specifying that
the same ``anything'' must appear in each position.  This is why variables have
names.  For example,

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(QList(listOf(QSym("supervisor"),
                QVar("x"),
            QVar("x")),
        null)),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => no answers
@end example

@noindent
finds all people who supervise themselves (though there are no such assertions
in our sample data base).

The query

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("job"),
                QVar("x"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                    QVar("type")),
                    null),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x", "type"),
)
// => ?x = [Bitdiddle, Ben], ?type = wizard
//    ?x = [Hacker, Alyssa, P], ?type = programmer
//    ?x = [Fect, Cy, D], ?type = programmer
//    ?x = [Tweakit, Lem, E], ?type = technician
@end example

@noindent
matches all job entries whose third item is a two-element list whose first item
is @code{computer}:

@noindent
The fixed-shape query above does not match Louis Reasoner's three-element
job title: its nested list pattern is a closed two-element list.  An open
tail variable accepts the remaining suffix:

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("job"),
                QVar("person"),
                QList(
                    listOf(QSym("computer"),
                        QVar("type")),
                    QVar("rest"),
                ),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("person", "type", "rest"),
)
@end example

@noindent
The tail binds to the unconsumed suffix: an empty list for two-element job
titles and a one-element list for Louis Reasoner's trailing @code{trainee}.
This is ordinary @code{QList} unification; a guard is not needed.


We can describe the query language's processing of simple queries as follows:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The system finds all assignments to variables in the query pattern that
@newterm{satisfy} the pattern---that is, all sets of values for the variables
such that if the pattern variables are @newterm{instantiated with} (replaced
by) the values, the result is in the data base.

@item
The system responds to the query by listing all instantiations of the query
pattern with the variable assignments that satisfy it.

@end itemize

@noindent
Note that if the pattern has no variables, the query reduces to a determination
of whether that pattern is in the data base.  If so, the empty assignment,
which assigns no values to variables, satisfies that pattern for that data
base.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.55}Exercise 4.55:} Give simple queries that retrieve
the following information from the data base:

@enumerate

@item
all people supervised by Ben Bitdiddle;

@item
the names and jobs of all people in the accounting division;

@item
the names and addresses of all people who live in Slumerville.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@subsubheading Compound queries

Simple queries form the primitive operations of the query language.  In order
to form compound operations, the query language provides means of combination.
One thing that makes the query language a logic programming language is that
the means of combination mirror the means of combination used in forming
logical expressions: @code{and}, @code{or}, and @code{not}.  These names are
not Kotlin calls; they are constructors and operations built into the query
language.

We can use @code{and} as follows to find the addresses of all the computer
programmers:

@example kotlin
ask(
    QAnd(
        listOf(
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("job"),
                        QVar("person"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                            QSym("programmer")), null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            QPattern(QList(listOf(QSym("address"),
                        QVar("person"),
                QVar("where")), null)),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("person", "where"),
)
// => ?person = [Hacker, Alyssa, P], ?where =
// [Cambridge, [Mass Ave],
    // 78]
// ?person = [Fect, Cy, D], ?where = [Cambridge, [Ames
// Street], 3]
@end example

@noindent
Each answer frame instantiates both patterns at once.
In general,

@example
(and ⟨@var{query₁}⟩ ⟨@var{query₂}⟩ @r{…} ⟨@var{queryₙ}⟩)
@end example
@noindent
is satisfied by all sets of values for the pattern variables that
simultaneously satisfy @math{{⟨\kern0.1em query_1⟩}} @r{…} @math{{⟨\kern0.1em query_n⟩}}.

As for simple queries, the system processes a compound query by finding all
assignments to the pattern variables that satisfy the query, then displaying
instantiations of the query with those values.

Another means of constructing compound queries is through @code{or}.  For
example,

@example kotlin
ask(
    QOr(
        listOf(
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("supervisor"),
                        QVar("x"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("Bitdiddle"),
                            QSym("Ben")),
                            null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("supervisor"),
                        QVar("x"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("Hacker"),
                                QSym("Alyssa"),
                            QSym("P")), null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => ?x = [Hacker, Alyssa, P]
//    ?x = [Fect, Cy, D]
//    ?x = [Tweakit, Lem, E]
//    ?x = [Reasoner, Louis]
@end example

@noindent
will find all employees supervised by Ben Bitdiddle or by Alyssa P. Hacker.

@noindent
In general,

@example
(or ⟨@var{query₁}⟩ ⟨@var{query₂}⟩ @r{…} ⟨@var{queryₙ}⟩)
@end example
@noindent
is satisfied by all sets of values for the pattern variables that satisfy at
least one of @math{{⟨\kern0.1em query_1⟩}} @r{…} @math{{⟨\kern0.1em query_n⟩}}.

Compound queries can also be formed with @code{not}. For example,

@example kotlin
ask(
    QAnd(
        listOf(
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("supervisor"),
                        QVar("x"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("Bitdiddle"),
                            QSym("Ben")),
                            null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            QNot(
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(
                            QSym("job"),
                            QVar("x"),
                            QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                                QSym("technician")),
                                    null),
                        ),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => ?x = [Hacker, Alyssa, P]
//    ?x = [Fect, Cy, D]
@end example

@noindent
finds all people supervised by Ben Bitdiddle who are not computer programmers.
In general,

@example
(not ⟨@var{query₁}⟩)
@end example
@noindent
is satisfied by all assignments to the pattern variables that do not satisfy
@math{{⟨\kern0.1em query_1⟩}}.@footnote{Actually, this description of @code{not} is valid
only for simple cases.  The real behavior of @code{not} is more complex.  We
will examine @code{not}'s peculiarities in @ref{4.4.2} and
@ref{4.4.3}.}

The final combining form is the @code{QGuard} host predicate.  A guard runs
a host predicate once its argument terms are bound.  In general,

@example kotlin
QGuard(
    ⟨@var{predicate over the bound terms}⟩,
    ⟨@var{argument terms}⟩,
)
@end example
@noindent
is satisfied by assignments to the pattern variables for which the predicate
over the bound argument terms is true.  For example, to find all people whose
salary is greater than $30,000 we could write@footnote{A guard should be used
only to perform an operation not provided in the query language.  In
particular, it should not be used to test equality (since that is what the
matching in the query language is designed to do) or inequality (since that can
be done with the @code{same} rule shown below).}

@example kotlin
fun salaryAbove(amount: QTerm, limit: Long): Boolean @{
    val number = (amount as? QSym)?.name?.toLongOrNull() ?: return false
    return number > limit
@}

ask(
    QAnd(
        listOf(
            QPattern(QList(listOf(QSym("salary"),
                        QVar("person"),
                QVar("amount")), null)),
            QGuard(@{ terms -> salaryAbove(terms.get(0),
                30000L) @},
                listOf(QVar("amount"))),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("person", "amount"),
)
// => ?person = [Bitdiddle, Ben], ?amount = 60000
//    ?person = [Hacker, Alyssa, P], ?amount = 40000
//    ?person = [Fect, Cy, D], ?amount = 35000
//    ?person = [Warbucks, Oliver], ?amount = 150000
//    ?person = [Scrooge, Eben], ?amount = 75000
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.56}Exercise 4.56:} Formulate compound queries that
retrieve the following information:

@enumerate a

@item
the names of all people who are supervised by Ben Bitdiddle, together with
their addresses;

@item
all people whose salary is less than Ben Bitdiddle's, together with their
salary and Ben Bitdiddle's salary;

@item
all people who are supervised by someone who is not in the computer division,
together with the supervisor's name and job.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@subsubheading Rules

In addition to primitive queries and compound queries, the query language
provides means for abstracting queries.  These are given by @newterm{rules}.
The rule

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(listOf(QSym("lives-near"),
                QVar("person-1"),
            QVar("person-2")), null),
        QAnd(
            listOf(
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(QSym("address"),
                            QVar("person-1"),
                            QVar("addr-1")),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(QSym("address"),
                            QVar("person-2"),
                            QVar("addr-2")),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
                QGuard(
                    @{ terms -> headName(terms.get(0)) ==
                        headName(terms.get(1)) @},
                    listOf(QVar("addr-1"),
                        QVar("addr-2")),
                ),
                QNot(
                    QPattern(
                        QList(listOf(QSym("same"),
                                QVar("person-1"),
                            QVar("person-2")), null),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
The rule binds each person's whole address row, then uses @code{QGuard} to
compare the town heads.  Open-tail patterns could expose these fields
directly; the guard here illustrates a host predicate over bound terms.  The
final @code{QNot} clause prevents the rule from saying that all people live
near themselves.

@noindent
The @code{same} relation is defined by a very simple
rule:@footnote{Notice that we do not need @code{same} in order to make two
things be the same: We just use the same pattern variable for each---in effect,
we have one thing instead of two things in the first place.  For example, see
@code{?x} twice in the @code{same} rule and @code{?middle-manager} in the
@code{wheel} rule below.  @code{Same} is useful when we want to force two
things to be different, such as @code{?person-1} and @code{?person-2} in the
@code{lives-near} rule.  Although using the same pattern variable in two parts
of a query forces the same value to appear in both places, using different
pattern variables does not force different values to appear.  (The values
assigned to different pattern variables may be the same or different.)}

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(listOf(QSym("same"),
                QVar("x"),
            QVar("x")),
            null),
        QAnd(emptyList()),
    ),
)
// a bodyless rule: the empty conjunction holds for any
// value of ?x
@end example

@noindent
The following rule declares that a person is a ``wheel'' in an organization if
he supervises someone who is in turn a supervisor:

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(listOf(QSym("wheel"),
            QVar("person")),
            null),
        QAnd(
            listOf(
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(QSym("supervisor"),
                            QVar("middle-manager"),
                                QVar("person")),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(QSym("supervisor"),
                            QVar("x"),
                            QVar("middle-manager")),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
The general form of a rule is the @code{QRule} constructor: a conclusion term
and a body query.

@example kotlin
QRule(⟨@var{conclusion term}⟩, ⟨@var{body query}⟩)
@end example
@noindent
where the conclusion is a @code{QTerm} pattern and the body is any
@code{QQuery}.@footnote{We will also allow rules with an empty body, as in
@code{same}, and we will interpret such a rule to mean that the rule
conclusion is satisfied by any values of the variables.} We can think of a rule
as representing a large (even infinite) set of assertions, namely all
instantiations of the rule conclusion with variable assignments that satisfy
the rule body.  When we described simple queries (patterns), we said that an
assignment to variables satisfies a pattern if the instantiated pattern is in
the data base.  But the pattern needn't be explicitly in the data base as an
assertion.  It can be an implicit assertion implied by a rule.  For example,
the query

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("lives-near"),
                QVar("x"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("Bitdiddle"),
                    QSym("Ben")),
                    null),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => ?x = [Reasoner, Louis]
//    ?x = [Aull, DeWitt]
@end example

@noindent
To find all computer programmers who live near Ben Bitdiddle, we can ask

@example kotlin
ask(
    QAnd(
        listOf(
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("job"),
                        QVar("x"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                            QSym("programmer")), null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            QPattern(
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("lives-near"),
                        QVar("x"),
                        QList(listOf(QSym("Bitdiddle"),
                            QSym("Ben")),
                            null),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => no answers: no programmer shares Ben's town
@end example

@noindent
As in the case of compound procedures, rules can be used as parts of other
rules (as we saw with the @code{lives-near} rule above) or even be defined
recursively.  For instance, the rule

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(listOf(QSym("outranked-by"),
                QVar("staff-person"),
            QVar("boss")), null),
        QOr(
            listOf(
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(QSym("supervisor"),
                            QVar("staff-person"),
                            QVar("boss")),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
                QAnd(
                    listOf(
                        QPattern(
                            QList(
                                listOf(
                                    QSym("supervisor"),
                                    QVar("staff-person"),
                                    QVar("middle-manager"),
                                ),
                                null,
                            ),
                        ),
                        QPattern(
                            QList(
                                listOf(
                                    QSym("outranked-by"),
                                    QVar("middle-manager"),
                                    QVar("boss"),
                                ),
                                null,
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
says that a staff person is outranked by a boss in the organization if the boss
is the person's supervisor or (recursively) if the person's supervisor is
outranked by the boss.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.57}Exercise 4.57:} Define a rule that says that
person 1 can replace person 2 if either person 1 does the same job as person 2
or someone who does person 1's job can also do person 2's job, and if person 1
and person 2 are not the same person. Using your rule, give queries that find
the following:

@enumerate a

@item
all people who can replace Cy D. Fect;

@item
all people who can replace someone who is being paid more than they are,
together with the two salaries.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.58}Exercise 4.58:} Define a rule that says that a
person is a ``big shot'' in a division if the person works in the division but
does not have a supervisor who works in the division.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.59}Exercise 4.59:} Ben Bitdiddle has missed one
meeting too many.  Fearing that his habit of forgetting meetings could cost him
his job, Ben decides to do something about it.  He adds all the weekly meetings
of the firm to the Microshaft data base by asserting the following:

@example kotlin
db.assertFact(
    QFact(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("meeting"),
                QSym("accounting"),
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("Monday"),
