@end example

Each of the above assertions is for a meeting of an entire division.  Ben also
adds an entry for the company-wide meeting that spans all the divisions.  All
of the company's employees attend this meeting.

@example kotlin
db.assertFact(
    QFact(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("meeting"),
                QSym("whole-company"),
                QList(
                    listOf(
                        QSym("Wednesday"),
                        QSym("4pm"),
                    ),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
On Friday morning, Ben wants to query the data base for all the meetings that
occur that day.  What query should he use?

@item
Alyssa P. Hacker is unimpressed.  She thinks it would be much more useful to be
able to ask for her meetings by specifying her name.  So she designs a rule
that says that a person's meetings include all @code{whole-company} meetings
plus all meetings of that person's division.  Fill in the body of Alyssa's
rule.

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("meeting-time"),
                QVar("person"),
                QVar("day-and-time"),
            ),
            null,
        ),
        QOr(
            listOf(
                QPattern(
                    QList(
                        listOf(
                            QSym("meeting"),
                            QSym("whole-company"),
                            QVar("day-and-time"),
                        ),
                        null,
                    ),
                ),
                QAnd(
                    listOf(
                        QPattern(
                            QList(
                                listOf(
                                    QSym("meeting"),
                                    QVar("division"),
                                    QVar("day-and-time"),
                                ),
                                null,
                            ),
                        ),
                        QPattern(
                            QList(
                                listOf(
                                    QSym("job"),
                                    QVar("person"),
                                    QVar("kind"),
                                ),
                                null,
                            ),
                        ),
                        QGuard(
                            @{ terms ->
                                headName(terms.get(0)) ==
                                    headName(terms.get(1))
                            @},
                            listOf(
                                QVar("division"),
                                QVar("kind"),
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
The division guard compares the heads of two already-bound terms.  Their
different row lengths do not require a fixed-shape pattern; open tails could
also expose the fields directly.

@item
Alyssa arrives at work on Wednesday morning and wonders what meetings she has
to attend that day.  Having defined the above rule, what query should she make
to find this out?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.60}Exercise 4.60:} By giving the query

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("lives-near"),
                QVar("person"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("Hacker"),
                        QSym("Alyssa"),
                    QSym("P")),
                    null),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("person"),
)
@end example
Alyssa P. Hacker is able to find people who live near her, with whom she can
ride to work.  On the other hand, when she tries to find all pairs of people
who live near each other by querying

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(listOf(QSym("lives-near"),
                QVar("person-1"),
            QVar("person-2")), null),
    ),
    listOf("person-1", "person-2"),
)
@end example
@noindent
she notices that each pair of people who live near each other is listed twice.
Why does this happen?  Is there a way to find a list of people who live near
each other, in which each pair appears only once?  Explain.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Logic as programs

We can regard a rule as a kind of logical implication: @emph{If} an assignment
of values to pattern variables satisfies the body, @emph{then} it satisfies the
conclusion.  Consequently, we can regard the query language as having the
ability to perform @newterm{logical deductions} based upon the rules.  As an
example, consider the @code{append} operation described at the beginning of
@ref{4.4}.  As we said, @code{append} can be characterized by the
following two rules:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For any list @code{y}, the empty list and @code{y} @code{append} to form
@code{y}.

@item
For any @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{y}, and @code{z}, @code{(cons u v)} and
@code{y} @code{append} to form @code{(cons u z)} if @code{v} and @code{y}
@code{append} to form @code{z}.

@end itemize

The host data model supports the recursive append relation directly.  The
tail variables in these @code{QList} terms match list suffixes:

@example kotlin
val appendBase = QRule(
    QList(
        listOf(
            QSym("append"),
            QList(emptyList(), null),
            QVar("y"),
            QVar("y"),
        ),
        null,
    ),
    QAnd(emptyList()),
)

val appendStep = QRule(
    QList(
        listOf(
            QSym("append"),
            QList(listOf(QVar("u")), QVar("v")),
            QVar("y"),
            QList(listOf(QVar("u")), QVar("z")),
        ),
        null,
    ),
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(QSym("append"),
                QVar("v"),
                QVar("y"),
                QVar("z")),
            null,
        ),
    ),
)
db.addRule(appendBase)
db.addRule(appendStep)
@end example

@noindent
Because the rules are relational, the same definition can solve for a missing
second list when the first list and result are known:

@example kotlin
val result = ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QSym("append"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("a"),
                    QSym("b")),
                    null),
                QVar("y"),
                QList(
                    listOf(QSym("a"),
                        QSym("b"),
                        QSym("c"),
                        QSym("d")),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("y"),
)
@end example

The expected answer is @code{?y = [c, d]}.  @code{QGuard} remains useful for
host predicates over fully bound terms, but it is not a substitute for
structural rules that must generate unknown values.

@noindent
The query system may seem to exhibit quite a bit of intelligence in using the
rules to deduce the answers to the queries above.  Actually, as we will see in
the next section, the system is following a well-determined algorithm in
unraveling the rules.  Unfortunately, although the system works impressively
in the fixed-shape cases, the general methods may break down in more complex
cases, as we will see in @ref{4.4.3}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.61}Exercise 4.61:} The following rules implement a
@code{next-to} relation that finds adjacent elements of a list:

@example kotlin
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QVar("x"),
                QSym("next-to"),
                QVar("y"),
                QSym("in"),
                QList(listOf(QVar("x"),
                    QVar("y")),
                    QVar("u")),
            ),
            null,
        ),
        QAnd(emptyList()),
    ),
)
db.addRule(
    QRule(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QVar("x"),
                QSym("next-to"),
                QVar("y"),
                QSym("in"),
                QList(listOf(QVar("v")), QVar("z")),
            ),
            null,
        ),
        QPattern(
            QList(
                listOf(
                    QVar("x"),
                    QSym("next-to"),
                    QVar("y"),
                    QSym("in"),
                    QVar("z"),
                ),
                null,
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
The following queries test matching with both closed lists and open tails.
Use the common-prefix and suffix rules of @code{unifyList} to explain each
answer:

@example kotlin
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QVar("x"),
                QSym("next-to"),
                QVar("y"),
                QSym("in"),
                QList(
                    listOf(QSym("1"),
                                QList(listOf(QSym("2"),
                        QSym("3")), null), QSym("4")),
                    null,
                ),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x", "y"),
)
ask(
    QPattern(
        QList(
            listOf(
                QVar("x"),
                QSym("next-to"),
                QSym("1"),
                QSym("in"),
                QList(listOf(QSym("2"),
                        QSym("1"),
                        QSym("3"),
                    QSym("1")), null),
            ),
            null,
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
@end example

@noindent
Explain each answer, including the suffix bound to the tail variable.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.62}Exercise 4.62:} Define rules to implement the
@code{last-pair} operation of @ref{Exercise 2.17}, which returns a list
containing the last element of a nonempty list.  Test the rules on finite
queries such as @code{(last-pair (3) ?x)}, @code{(last-pair (1 2 3) ?x)},
and @code{(last-pair (2 ?x) (3))}.  What happens on
@code{(last-pair ?x (3))}?  Explain what an unbound first argument lets the
search construct and how rule-depth limits affect the answers.
@end quotation

@quotation
