@subsection Implementing the Query System

Section @ref{4.4.2} described how the query system works. Now we fill in the
details by presenting a complete implementation of the system.

@menu
* 4.4.4.1::          The Driver Loop and Instantiation
* 4.4.4.2::          The Evaluator
* 4.4.4.3::          Finding Assertions by Pattern Matching
* 4.4.4.4::          Rules and Unification
* 4.4.4.5::          Maintaining the Data Base
* 4.4.4.6::          Stream Operations
* 4.4.4.7::          Query Syntax Procedures
* 4.4.4.8::          Frames and Bindings
@end menu

@node 4.4.4.1, 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4, 4.4.4
@subsubsection The Driver Loop and Instantiation

There is no read loop: adding to the data base is a host call
(@code{assertFact} for an assertion, @code{addRule} for a rule), and asking a
question is a host call to @code{run} on one of the three driver modes.  The
driver runs the query against an initial empty frame.  The result of the
evaluation is a sequence of frames generated by satisfying the query with
variable values found in the data base.  Each frame renders the asked
variables through @code{renderAnswer}:

@example kotlin
public fun run(query: QQuery,
    variables: List<QVar>): Sequence<QFrame> =
    sequence @{
        val answers = queryFrames(query,
            QFrame(emptyMap()),
            0)
        yieldAll(
            if (deduplicating) answers.distinctBy @{
                renderAnswer(it, variables)
            @} else answers,
        )
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Here, as in the other evaluators in this chapter, the abstract syntax
@emph{is} the host constructors of @ref{4.4.1}: there is no text to parse
and no @code{assert!} command to recognize.  @code{QVar("x")} is the pattern
variable the book writes @code{?x}, used as written everywhere.

To instantiate an answer, the driver resolves each asked variable in the
frame, following chains of bound variables; a variable with no binding
renders as itself.  The rendering is pinned: @code{QSym} renders its name,
@code{QVar} renders @code{?name}, a proper @code{QList} renders
@code{[a, b, c]}, an improper one @code{[a, b | rest]}, and an answer line is
@code{?name = <rendered term>}:

@example kotlin
public fun renderAnswer(
    frame: QFrame,
    variables: List<QVar>,
): List<String> =
    variables.map @{ variable ->
        "?$@{variable.name@} = $@{renderTerm(reify(variable, frame))@}"
    @}
@end example

@noindent
The frame is an immutable map of variables to terms, manipulated as described
in @ref{4.4.4.8}.

@node 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4.1, 4.4.4
@subsubsection The Evaluator

@code{queryFrames} dispatches over the sealed @code{QQuery} family.  Each
branch returns a lazy @code{Sequence<QFrame>}:

@example kotlin
private fun queryFrames(
    query: QQuery,
    frame: QFrame,
    depth: Int,
): Sequence<QFrame> =
    when (query) @{
        is QPattern -> patternFrames(query.term,
            frame,
            depth)
        is QAnd ->
            query.parts.fold(sequenceOf(frame)) @{ frames,
                part ->
                frames.flatMap @{ queryFrames(part,
                    it,
                    depth) @}
            @}
        is QOr -> interleave(query.parts,
            frame,
            depth)
        is QNot -> sequence @{
            if (!queryFrames(
                query.part,
                frame,
                depth,
            ).iterator().hasNext()) @{
                yield(frame)
            @}
        @}
        is QGuard -> sequence @{
            val bound = query.args.map @{ reify(it,
                frame) @}
            if (bound.none @{ containsVariable(it) @} &&
                query.predicate(bound)
            ) yield(frame)
        @}
        is QUnique -> sequence @{
            val matches =
                queryFrames(query.part,
                    frame,
                    depth).iterator()
            if (matches.hasNext()) @{
                val only = matches.next()
                if (!matches.hasNext()) yield(only)
            @}
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Disjunction merges its branch streams with @code{interleave}:

@example kotlin
private fun interleave(
    parts: List<QQuery>,
    frame: QFrame,
    depth: Int,
): Sequence<QFrame> =
    sequence @{
        val pending = ArrayDeque(
            parts.map @{
                queryFrames(it, frame,
                    depth).iterator()
            @})
        while (pending.isNotEmpty()) @{
            val current = pending.removeFirst()
            if (current.hasNext()) @{
                yield(current.next())
                pending.addLast(current)
            @}
        @}
    @}
@end example

@subsubheading Simple queries

The @code{patternFrames} sequence yields matching facts first, then matching
rules, each in database order.  It does not materialize every answer before
the consumer requests one.

@subsubheading Compound queries

@code{QAnd} folds left to right: each sequence of frames from one part feeds
the next part.  An empty conjunction yields its incoming frame, as required
for a bodyless rule.  @code{QOr} runs each disjunct against the same incoming
frame and merges the answer streams with @code{interleave}: it takes one
answer from the first disjunct, then one from the next, and so on around the
list, dropping a disjunct once its stream is exhausted.  A disjunct that
answers forever therefore cannot keep a later disjunct from answering; a
disjunct that neither answers nor finishes still stalls the turn it is
waiting on.

@subsubheading Filters

@code{QNot} includes an incoming frame only when its subquery has no first
answer.  A @code{QGuard} reifies its arguments and runs the host predicate
only when every argument is ground; otherwise it yields no frame.  The
predicate is an ordinary host function value carried by the query, not an
operation-table entry.  @code{QUnique} yields its subquery's answer only when
there is exactly one; it inspects at most two answers to decide.

@node 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4.2, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Finding Assertions by Pattern Matching

Pattern matching considers facts with the same known head symbol before
calling the unifier.  The database filters its chronological fact list for
each query; a variable or empty-list head has no known symbol and searches all
facts.  The filter avoids unnecessary unification but does not use a separate
index.

@example kotlin
private fun unify(
    pattern: QTerm,
    datum: QTerm,
    frame: QFrame,
): QFrame? @{
    val left = if (pattern is QVar) reify(pattern,
        frame) else pattern
    val right = if (datum is QVar) reify(datum,
        frame) else datum
    return when @{
        left is QVar -> frame.extend(left, right)
        right is QVar -> frame.extend(right, left)
        left is QSym && right is QSym ->
            if (left.name == right.name) frame else null
        left is QList &&
            right is QList -> unifyList(left,
            right,
            frame)
        else -> null
    @}
@}

private fun unifyList(
    left: QList,
    right: QList,
    frame: QFrame,
): QFrame? @{
    val common = minOf(left.items.size,
        right.items.size)
    var current = frame
    for (index in 0 until common) @{
        current = unify(left.items[index],
            right.items[index],
            current)
            ?: return null
    @}
    if (left.items.size > common) @{
        val tail = right.tail ?: return null
        return unify(
            tail,
            QList(left.items.drop(common), left.tail),
            current,
        )
    @}
    if (right.items.size > common) @{
        val tail = left.tail ?: return null
        return unify(
            tail,
            QList(right.items.drop(common), right.tail),
            current,
        )
    @}
    if (left.tail == null &&
        right.tail == null) return current
    return unify(
        left.tail ?: QList(emptyList(), null),
        right.tail ?: QList(emptyList(), null),
        current,
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
Unification returns a new immutable @code{QFrame} on a match and null on a
mismatch.  Before comparison, @code{reify} follows any existing bindings;
extending a frame checks for an occurrence cycle and copies its binding map.
If one list has more explicit items, the shorter list must have a tail that
can unify with the unmatched suffix.  Equal-length prefixes unify their tail
terms last, treating a missing tail as the empty proper list.

@subsubheading Patterns with tails

A @code{QList} tail is ordinary pattern data, not dotted-list reader syntax.
For example, a pattern with items @code{computer} and @code{?type} and tail
@code{?rest} matches both @code{[computer, programmer]} and
@code{[computer, programmer, trainee]}; @code{?rest} receives the remaining
suffix.  Closed lists still require matching lengths.  Relational rules can
use such tail variables directly; @code{QGuard} is reserved for host
predicates that the structural query language does not express.

@node 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4.3, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Rules and Unification

Rules are the rule analog of facts.  For one pattern the engine unifies
against the facts with the same head, then against the rules with the same
head (@ref{4.4.4.5}); past the driver's depth bound only the facts are
tried.  Each rule whose conclusion unifies runs its body in the unified
frame, one level deeper:

@example kotlin
private fun patternFrames(
    pattern: QTerm,
    frame: QFrame,
    depth: Int,
): Sequence<QFrame> = sequence @{
    val head = headOf(pattern)
    for (fact in db.factsWithHead(head)) @{
        unify(pattern,
            fact.term,
            frame)?.let @{ yield(it) @}
    @}
    if (depth >= maxDepth) return@@sequence
    for (unrenamed in db.rulesWithHead(head)) @{
        val rule = freshRule(unrenamed)
        val matched = unify(pattern,
            rule.conclusion,
            frame) ?: continue
        yieldAll(queryFrames(rule.body,
                matched,
            depth + 1))
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The engine creates a fresh version of each rule's variables for every
application, so a rule's @code{?x} cannot capture a query's @code{?x}.
The unifier reifies both sides and accepts a binding only when it is
consistent with the frame.  @code{QFrame.extend} performs an occurs check:
a variable cannot be bound to a term that contains that same variable.

@node 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4.4, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Maintaining the Data Base

The database stores facts and rules in chronological insertion order.  For
a pattern with a known head symbol, the driver filters that list to candidate
entries before unifying; a variable or empty-list head scans every entry.
This is a linear filter, not a precomputed head index.

@example kotlin
public class QueryDatabase @{
    private val facts = mutableListOf<QFact>()
    private val rules = mutableListOf<QRule>()

    public fun assertFact(fact: QFact) @{
        facts.add(fact)
    @}

    public fun addRule(rule: QRule) @{
        rules.add(rule)
    @}

    internal fun factsWithHead(head: String): List<QFact> =
        if (head == "?") facts.toList() else facts.filter @{
            headOf(it.term) == head
        @}

    internal fun rulesWithHead(head: String): List<QRule> =
        if (head == "?") rules.toList() else rules.filter @{
            headOf(it.conclusion) == head
        @}
@}

private fun headOf(term: QTerm): String =
    when (term) @{
        is QSym -> term.name
        is QVar -> "?"
        is QList -> term.items.firstOrNull()?.let(::headOf) ?: "?"
    @}
@end example

@noindent
A head that is a variable, or an empty list, indexes under @code{"?"}, so a
pattern with a constant head tries only same-head entries while a
variable-headed pattern tries the @code{"?"} bucket.  This folds the book's
@code{fetch} and @code{get-stream} layer into two small filters; the
chronology guarantee is what keeps the pinned transcripts in insertion
order (see @ref{Exercise 4.70}).

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.70}Exercise 4.70:} In the original query engine,
the current assertion or rule stream was captured in a local variable before
a new stream was installed.  This edition's @code{QueryDatabase} instead
stores facts and rules in chronological @code{MutableList}s and adds entries
in place.

Why would saving a reference to one of these mutable lists not preserve a
snapshot of its old contents?  Contrast this with a persistent lazy stream,
where the old tail can be captured before the new stream is formed.  Use the
infinite-stream-of-ones example of @ref{3.5.2} as a thought experiment: which
version can accidentally refer to the stream being constructed rather than
the previous stream?
@end quotation

@node 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4.7, 4.4.4.5, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Stream Operations

@code{queryFrames} and @code{patternFrames} return lazy
@code{Sequence<QFrame>} values.  Conjunctions compose those sequences with
@code{flatMap}; disjunctions merge them with @code{interleave}, taking one
answer from each disjunct in turn.  The driver yields answers on demand, so a
consumer can take a prefix without constructing every match.

The search is still depth-first except across the disjuncts of one
disjunction.  An infinite earlier conjunct can prevent a later conjunct from
being reached, and a left-recursive rule can diverge before an answer is
available.  Lazy sequences avoid eager materialization; interleaving keeps
every disjunct moving, but neither guarantees fairness across a whole query
nor termination.

@node 4.4.4.7, 4.4.4.8, 4.4.4.6, 4.4.4

@subsubsection Query Syntax Procedures

There is no syntax layer to implement: the host constructors @emph{are} the
syntax.  A query is a @code{QQuery} value (@code{QPattern}, @code{QAnd},
@code{QOr}, @code{QNot}, @code{QGuard}, @code{QUnique}), a rule is a
@code{QRule} of a conclusion term and a body query, and a fact is a
@code{QFact} over @code{QTerm} data (@code{QSym}, @code{QVar},
@code{QList}).  Dispatch needs no @code{type} tag or operator table: the
engine's @code{when} over the sealed @code{QQuery} family (@ref{4.4.4.2})
is the whole dispatch.

In particular there is no reader to teach and no @code{assert!} command to
recognize: @code{db.assertFact} and @code{db.addRule} (@ref{4.4.4.5}) add to
the data base directly.  Pattern variables need no internal
@code{(? symbol)} encoding and no @code{querySyntaxProcess} pass:
@code{QVar("x")} is written, matched, and rendered as @code{?x} everywhere.
Constant symbols for database indexing (@ref{4.4.4.5}) are @code{QSym}
values.  Each rule application uses @code{freshRule} to give its variables
new names, so separate applications cannot capture one another's bindings.
Answer instantiation uses the pinned rendering of @ref{4.4.4.1}; unbound
asked variables render as themselves.

@node 4.4.4.8, Chapter 5, 4.4.4.7, 4.4.4
@subsubsection Frames and Bindings

Frames are immutable maps from variables to terms:

@example kotlin
public data class QFrame(val bindings: Map<QVar, QTerm>)
@end example

@noindent
Resolving follows chains of bound variables to their values; extending
copies the map with one more binding.  A stored binding is never modified,
and no variable is ever bound twice.  Failed matching answers no frame,
never a marker value.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.71}Exercise 4.71:} Louis Reasoner wonders why
@code{patternFrames} answers facts before rules, and why the driver's
@code{run} exposes its answers as a lazy @code{Sequence<QFrame>} rather than
answering an eager list.  Can you give examples of queries where answering
rules before facts, or forcing every answer eagerly, would lead to undesirable
behavior?  (Hint: think of a rule with infinitely many answers, and of a
consumer that takes only the first few.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.72}Exercise 4.72:} The engine merges disjunct
answers with an interleaving process so that every disjunct makes progress
even when an earlier one answers forever (@ref{4.4.4.6}).  Give examples that
illustrate why interleaving works better than appending the disjunct streams.
(Hint: why did we use @code{interleave} in @ref{3.5.3}?)  Can you construct a
query on which appending the disjuncts' answers would never reach the second
disjunct, while the engine's interleaving does?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.73}Exercise 4.73:} The query engine traverses
lazy @code{Sequence} values, composing conjunctions with depth-first
@code{flatMap}; it does not materialize the complete answer list before a
consumer asks for it.  Explain why this does not guarantee fair search for a
left-recursive rule or prevent an infinite branch from delaying later
answers.  Where the engine does interleave, in a disjunction, how does that
change the observed answer order and termination behavior?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.74}Exercise 4.74:} Alyssa P. Hacker observes
that negation, guards, and fact matching map each frame to either no frames
or a single frame, so no interleaving is needed when combining those answers.

@enumerate a

@item
Write Alyssa's combined helper over answer lists: map each frame to its
zero-or-one answers and concatenate.

@item
Does the query system's behavior change if we change it in this way?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.75}Exercise 4.75:} The query language admits
@code{QUnique}: it succeeds for exactly one match.  For example,

@example kotlin
ask(
    QUnique(
        QPattern(
            QList(
                listOf(
                    QSym("job"),
                    QVar("x"),
                    QList(listOf(QSym("computer"),
                        QSym("wizard")),
                        null),
                ),
                null,
            ),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x"),
)
// => ?x = [Bitdiddle, Ben]
@end example
@noindent
since Ben is the only computer wizard, and the same query with
@code{(computer programmer)} answers nothing, since there is more than one
computer programmer.  Moreover,

@example kotlin
ask(
    QAnd(
        listOf(
            QPattern(QList(listOf(QSym("job"),
                        QVar("x"),
                    QVar("j")),
                null)),
            QUnique(
                QPattern(QList(listOf(QSym("job"),
                            QVar("anyone"),
                    QVar("j")), null)),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    listOf("x", "j"),
)
@end example
@noindent
lists all the jobs that are filled by only one person, and the people who
fill them.  No dispatch table is involved: @code{QUnique} is a sealed
@code{QQuery} variant the engine's @code{when} handles directly.

Test your understanding by forming a query that lists all people who
supervise precisely one person.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.76}Exercise 4.76:} Our implementation of @code{and}
as a series combination of queries (@ref{Figure 4.5}) is elegant, but it is
inefficient because in processing the second query of the @code{and} we must
scan the data base for each frame produced by the first query.  If the data
base has @math{n} elements, and a typical query produces a number of output frames
proportional to @math{n} (say @math{{n \,/\, k}}), then scanning the data base for each
frame produced by the first query will require @math{{n^2 /\, k}} calls to the
pattern matcher.  Another approach would be to process the two clauses of the
@code{and} separately, then look for all pairs of output frames that are
compatible.  If each query produces @math{{n \,/\, k}} output frames, then this means
that we must perform @math{{n^2 /\, k^2}} compatibility checks---a factor of @math{k}
fewer than the number of matches required in our current method.

Devise an implementation of @code{and} that uses this strategy.  You must
implement a procedure that takes two @code{QFrame} values as inputs, checks
whether the bindings in the frames are compatible, and, if so, produces a
frame that merges the two sets of bindings.  This operation is similar to
unification.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.77}Exercise 4.77:} In @ref{4.4.3} we saw
that @code{QNot} and @code{QGuard} can cause the query language to give
``wrong'' answers if these filtering operations are applied to frames in which
variables are unbound.  Devise a way to fix this shortcoming.  One idea is to
perform the filtering in a ``delayed'' manner by appending to the frame a
``promise'' to filter that is fulfilled only when enough variables have been
bound to make the operation possible.  We could wait to perform filtering until
all other operations have been performed.  However, for efficiency's sake, we
would like to perform filtering as soon as possible so as to cut down on the
number of intermediate frames generated.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.78}Exercise 4.78:} Redesign the query language as a
nondeterministic program to be implemented using the search evaluator of
@ref{4.3}, rather than as an answer-sequence process.  In this approach, each
run's value is a single answer (rather than the sequence of all answers),
and re-running with a resumed search produces more answers.  You should find
that much of the mechanism we built in this section is subsumed by
nondeterministic search and backtracking.  You will probably also find,
however, that your new query language has subtle differences in behavior from
the one implemented here.  Can you find examples that illustrate this
difference?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.79}Exercise 4.79:} When we implemented the guest
evaluator in @ref{4.1}, we saw how to use local environments to avoid
name conflicts between the parameters of procedures.  For example, in
evaluating

@example kotlin
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x

fun sumOfSquares(x: Long,
    y: Long): Long = square(x) + square(y)

fun main() @{
    println(sumOfSquares(3L, 4L))
@}
// 25
@end example
@noindent
there is no confusion between the @code{x} in @code{square} and the @code{x} in
@code{sumOfSquares}, because we evaluate the body of each procedure in an
environment that is specially constructed to contain bindings for the local
variables.  The query engine avoids conflicts by using @code{freshRule} to
rename rule variables for every application.  An alternative implementation
could retain rule-variable identities in a separate environment, analogous to
the local environments that the guest evaluator uses for procedure calls.

Implement that environment-based alternative for the query language, preserving
the current engine's rule-scoping behavior.  Compare it with the existing
freshening pass and explore how rule-local environments might support larger
systems, such as block-structured rules.  Can you relate this to making
deductions in a context (for example, ``If I supposed that @math{P} were true,
then I would be able to deduce @math{A} and @math{B}.'') as a method of
problem solving?  (This problem is open-ended.  A good answer is probably
worth a Ph.D.)
@end quotation

@node    Chapter 5, 5.1, 4.4, Top
@chapter Computing with Register Machines

@quotation
My aim is to show that the heavenly machine is not a kind of divine, live
being, but a kind of clockwork (and he who believes that a clock has soul
attributes the maker's glory to the work), insofar as nearly all the manifold
motions are caused by a most simple and material force, just as all motions of
the clock are caused by a single weight.

---Johannes Kepler (letter to Herwart von Hohenburg, 1605)
@end quotation


@noindent
We began this book by studying processes and by describing processes in terms
of procedures written in the guest language.  To explain the meanings of
these procedures, we used a succession of models of evaluation: the
substitution model of @ref{Chapter 1}, the environment model of
@ref{Chapter 3}, and the metacircular evaluator of @ref{Chapter 4}.  Our
examination of the metacircular evaluator, in particular, dispelled much of
the mystery of how guest languages are interpreted.  But even the
metacircular evaluator leaves important questions unanswered, because it
fails to elucidate the mechanisms of control in a guest evaluator.  For instance, the evaluator does not explain how the evaluation of a
subexpression manages to return a value to the expression that uses this value,
nor does the evaluator explain how some recursive procedures generate iterative
processes (that is, are evaluated using constant space) whereas other recursive
procedures generate recursive processes.  These questions remain unanswered
because the metacircular evaluator is itself a guest program and hence
inherits the control structure of the underlying evaluator.  In order to
provide a more complete description of the control structure of the guest
evaluator, we must work at a more primitive level than the guest language
itself.

In this chapter we will describe processes in terms of the step-by-step
operation of a traditional computer.  Such a computer, or @newterm{register machine}, 
sequentially executes @newterm{instructions} that manipulate the
contents of a fixed set of storage elements called @newterm{registers}.  A
typical register-machine instruction applies a primitive operation to the
contents of some registers and assigns the result to another register.  Our
descriptions of processes executed by register machines will look very much
like ``machine-language'' programs for traditional computers.  However, instead
of focusing on the machine language of any particular computer, we will examine
several guest procedures and design a specific register machine to execute each
procedure.  Thus, we will approach our task from the perspective of a hardware
architect rather than that of a machine-language computer programmer.  In
designing register machines, we will develop mechanisms for implementing
important programming constructs such as recursion.  We will also present a
language for describing designs for register machines.  In @ref{5.2} we
will implement a Kotlin program that uses these descriptions to simulate the
machines we design.

Most of the primitive operations of our register machines are very simple.  For
example, an operation might add the numbers fetched from two registers,
producing a result to be stored into a third register.  Such an operation can
be performed by easily described hardware.  In order to deal with list
structure, however, we will also use memory operations for heads, tails, and
pair construction, which require an elaborate storage-allocation
mechanism.  In @ref{5.3} we study their implementation in terms of more
elementary operations.

In @ref{5.4}, after we have accumulated experience formulating simple
procedures as register machines, we will design a machine that carries out the
algorithm described by the metacircular evaluator of @ref{4.1}.  This
will fill in the gap in our understanding of how guest expressions are
interpreted, by providing an explicit model for the mechanisms of control in
the evaluator.  In @ref{5.5} we will study a simple compiler that
translates guest programs into sequences of instructions that can be executed
directly with the registers and operations of the evaluator register machine.

